  </text>
  <heading level="3">
    <location value="60"/>
    <location value="318"/>
    <location value="120"/>
    <location value="328"/>
    Abstract
  </heading>
  <index>
    <location value="59"/>
    <location value="352"/>
    <location value="448"/>
    <location value="449"/>
    <fcel/>
    Computational modeling of cellular behavior - the virtual cell - has emerged as a stated grand
    <nl/>
    <fcel/>
    challenge at the intersection of artificial intelligence and biology, yet existing foundation models
    <nl/>
    <fcel/>
    remain specialized: single-cell models process dissociated transcriptomes only, spatial models
    <nl/>
    <fcel/>
    require dedicated spatial-aware architectures, and perturbation predictors depend on manually
    <nl/>
    <fcel/>
    curated knowledge bases that cap generalization. Here we introduce OCellus, a single nine-
    <nl/>
    <fcel/>
    billion-parameter language model (Qwen3.5-9B) fine-tuned on twenty-two biological tasks that
    <nl/>
  </index>
