  <list>
    <ldiv/>
    <location value="40"/>
    <location value="48"/>
    <location value="452"/>
    <location value="145"/>
    these four tasks demonstrate that multi-task language-model training produces a representation 1 that extends beyond cell-type identity and perturbation response into cross-species, cross2 developmental-stage, and gene-functional reasoning. Concurrent work such as CeLLM (Li et al., 3 2024), ST-LLM (Wang et al., 2024), and AlphaCell (2025) has begun exploring adjacent 4 directions; the present contribution is the breadth of tasks unified in a single fine-tuning run 5 rather than the paradigm itself. 6
  </list>
  <heading level="5">
    <location value="40"/>
    <location value="165"/>
    <location value="135"/>
    <location value="173"/>
    OCellus-Agent 7
  </heading>
  <text>
    <location value="35"/>
    <location value="193"/>
    <location value="452"/>
    <location value="362"/>
    Making the full capability suite accessible without writing custom code per task requires an 8 agent layer that translates natural-language queries into executable pipelines. OCellus-Agent 9 provides this through three architectural layers over the same Qwen3.5-9B backbone (Figure 8): 10 a Coordinator containing a trained Planner LoRA, a rule-based Router, and a three-layer Verifier 11 with a trained Critic LoRA; five expert roles (Data Loader, Annotator, Perturbation, Spatial, 12 Explainer) implemented as dynamic LoRA swaps on the shared backbone; and a Tool Registry 13 of fifteen registered tools exposed via a Gradio web interface. Six interchangeable runtime 14 configurations are loaded on demand through dynamic adapter swapping, so the entire agent 15 occupies approximately twenty-two gigabytes of GPU memory on a single GPU rather than five 16 separate model instances. 17
  </text>
