    <location value="332"/>
    graph, the rule-based Router dispatches each step to the appropriate expert, and the three-layer 1 Verifier provides biological plausibility checks before any result reaches the user. The 2 architectural choices reflect deliberate engineering trade-offs: a single-backbone design keeps the 3 system deployable on one GPU; rule-based routing avoids the brittleness of learned routers when 4 the tool inventory is closed; and the three-layer Verifier pattern absorbs the well-known failure 5 mode of language-model critics rubber-stamping bad outputs through balanced accept-reject 6 training data and explicit user-warning on retry exhaustion. The 75 percent task success rate 7 exceeds the 51 percent baseline of a flat ReAct loop on the same benchmark, suggesting that the 8 Coordinator-plus-Verifier architecture contributes to pipeline success; the sample size of eighty 9 trials limits statistical certainty, and head-to-head comparison against CellAgent and scAgents on 10 identical queries remains future work. Looking forward, we envision agents that autonomously 11 design perturbation experiments, integrate multi-modal data, and generate testable hypotheses. 12 Several limitations warrant mention. OCellus is based on Qwen3.5-9B; scaling to larger architectures may yield further improvements in both prediction accuracy and explanation specific network reconstruction could capture regulatory logic that static topology misses.
  </text>
