    LoRA-scale training because the optimizer state is already small. DoRA doubled training time
  </list>
  <text>
    <location value="40"/>
    <location value="48"/>
    <location value="170"/>
    <location value="74"/>
    1 for negligible quality gain. 2
  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="35"/>
    <location value="93"/>
    <location value="454"/>
    <location value="404"/>
    After Liger Kernel freed fifty-two gigabytes of GPU memory per GPU, we allocated the memory 3 budget across four candidate dimensions: increasing perdevice batch size (12 → 16 → 32), 4 increasing LoRA rank (8 → 32 → 64), unfreezing the embedding and language -modeling-head 5 layers, and DoRA. We evaluated the embedding-unfreezing option in detail because its forty6 five-gigabyte cost is non-trivial, measuring representation quality along three axes -gene -gene 7 functional similarity (Spearman correlation between embedding cosine similarity and shared 8 Gene Ontology annotation), cell-type clustering quality (k-nearest-neighbor accuracy, adjusted 9 Rand index, normalized mutual information on five thousand cells spanning two hundred 10 eighteen types), and short-answer task accuracy across eleven downstream tasks. Unfreezing 11 yields a 21 percent training-time cost and provides only a 1.8-percentage-point average accuracy 12 gain while consuming forty-five gigabytes of additional GPU memory. The interpretation is that 13 the language-modeling head benefits substantially from output calibration on biology-specific 14 labels, whereas the input embedding layer already receives adequate signal from the pretrained 15 vocabulary; the freed memory is therefore better spent on larger per-device batch size and higher 16 LoRA rank. The optimal configuration (per-device batch size thirty-two, gradient accumulation 17 two, sequence packing) yielded 6.17 seconds per training iteration, completing the full four-stage 18 pipeline in approximately 2.5 days. We release all exploration artifacts (matched-pair 19 checkpoints, evaluation scripts, per-task breakdowns) to support community follow-up work. 20
  </text>
